Mavergreen's own: clang treats aligned allocation and sized deallocation as available for every macOS
deployment target.

Why: clang marks the aligned operators new and delete unavailable below macOS 10.13, and sized delete
below 10.12, because the system's libc++ lacked them. This toolchain always links its own libc++
(libc++.a through clang++.cfg's portable-ld, or libcxx22), which defines them. Marked unavailable, an
over-aligned new or make_unique did not compile ("only available on macOS 10.13 or newer"), and
std::vector of an alignas(64) type silently got malloc's 16-byte alignment (200 of 200 misaligned on
10.9.5). Forcing them on with -faligned-allocation in the driver config instead broke C++03, where
clang's implicit std::align_val_t clashes with libc++'s; with the predicates false, each standard keeps
clang's own default (aligned allocation from C++17, sized deallocation from C++14). Mac Catalyst and the
other platforms are unchanged.

Not for upstream, whose Apple toolchains use the system's libc++.

tests/smoke-target.sh is the regression test (over-aligned allocation and sized delete with no flags
given, and C++03 headers still compiling).

--- a/clang/lib/Driver/ToolChains/Darwin.cpp
+++ b/clang/lib/Driver/ToolChains/Darwin.cpp
@@ -3111,9 +3111,10 @@
   if (isTargetMacCatalyst())
     return TargetVersion < alignedAllocMinVersion(llvm::Triple::MacOSX);
   switch (TargetPlatform) {
-  case MacOS: // Earlier than 10.13.
-    OS = llvm::Triple::MacOSX;
-    break;
+  case MacOS:
+    // This toolchain links its own libc++, which defines the aligned
+    // operators new and delete, whatever the deployment target.
+    return false;
   case IPhoneOS:
     OS = llvm::Triple::IOS;
     break;
@@ -3202,9 +3203,10 @@
   if (isTargetMacCatalyst())
     return TargetVersion < sizedDeallocMinVersion(llvm::Triple::MacOSX);
   switch (TargetPlatform) {
-  case MacOS: // Earlier than 10.12.
-    OS = llvm::Triple::MacOSX;
-    break;
+  case MacOS:
+    // This toolchain links its own libc++, which defines the sized
+    // operators delete, whatever the deployment target.
+    return false;
   case IPhoneOS:
     OS = llvm::Triple::IOS;
     break;
